{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>ורפא ירפא.</b> Throughout Scripture we do not find any act of healing performed by flesh and blood except with a dagesh (i.e., the pi'el form with geminated ר). And so (Jeremiah 51:9): ״רפאנו את בבל ולא נרפתה״. But with the Holy One, blessed be He, we find the weak (i.e., non-geminated) form, as it is written (Jeremiah 17:14): ״רפאני ה׳ וארפא״, and it is written (Psalms 147:3): ״הרופא לשבורי לב״, (Hosea 14:5): ״ארפא משובתם״, (Genesis 20:17): ״וירפא אלהים את אבימלך״, (Exodus 15:26): ״כי אני ה׳ רופאך״. The reason for this is that healing by flesh and blood is only through pain and exertion — one must endure the bitter medicine or potion — but the healing of the Holy One, blessed be He, comes with gentleness; there is no pain whatsoever, for the blessing of HaShem makes rich and adds no sorrow with it. And what our Rabbis of blessed memory said — ״ורפא ירפא, from here permission was given to the healer to heal״ — they said this only concerning an external wound, which is what the verse speaks of. But an internal illness is not dependent on the hand of the healer, but rather on the hand of the Healer of all flesh, in whose hand is the soul of all living."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:35.425378436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}